**(i) Any two assumptions:**

- the gas contains a very large number of identical molecules in continuous, random motion;
- the volume of the molecules is negligible compared with the volume of the container;
- the collisions between molecules, and with the walls, are perfectly **elastic**, so no kinetic energy is lost;
- there are no intermolecular forces except during collisions (so no potential energy);
- the duration of a collision is negligible compared with the time between collisions;
- Newton's laws of motion apply to the molecules.

**(ii)** $\overline{c^{2}}$ is the **mean square speed** of the molecules: the mean of the squares of the speeds of all the molecules. Its square root, $\sqrt{\overline{c^{2}}}$, is the root-mean-square (r.m.s.) speed.

